  "name": "GALACTOSEMIA CLASSICAL (TRANSFERASE) QUANTITATIVE",
  "code": "1107",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"133\" data-end=\"366\">The <strong data-start=\"137\" data-end=\"176\">Galactosemia Classical Quantitative</strong> test measures the activity of the enzyme <strong data-start=\"218\" data-end=\"268\">galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase (GALT)</strong>, which is essential in the metabolism of galactose (a sugar found in milk). This test is used to:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"368\" data-end=\"666\">\n<li data-start=\"368\" data-end=\"447\">\n<p data-start=\"370\" data-end=\"447\"><strong data-start=\"370\" data-end=\"403\">Diagnose classic galactosemia</strong>, an autosomal recessive metabolic disorder.</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"448\" data-end=\"507\">\n<p data-start=\"450\" data-end=\"507\">Identify <strong data-start=\"459\" data-end=\"471\">carriers</strong> or <strong data-start=\"475\" data-end=\"506\">partial enzyme deficiencies</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"508\" data-end=\"581\">\n<p data-start=\"510\" data-end=\"581\">Guide <strong data-start=\"516\" data-end=\"547\">newborn screening follow-up</strong> if galactose levels are elevated.</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"582\" data-end=\"666\">\n<p data-start=\"584\" data-end=\"666\">Prevent life-threatening complications by enabling <strong data-start=\"635\" data-end=\"665\">early dietary intervention</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Low or Undetectable GALT Activity</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Possible Indications</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Classic galactosemia</strong></td>\n<td>Complete or near-complete absence of GALT enzyme. Leads to accumulation of toxic galactose metabolites.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Symptoms</strong></td>\n<td>In infants: jaundice, vomiting, hepatomegaly, failure to thrive, lethargy, cataracts, and risk of sepsis.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Long-term risks</strong></td>\n<td>If untreated: developmental delays, speech problems, ovarian failure in females, and liver/kidney damage.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Carrier or Duarte variant</strong></td>\n<td>Partial enzyme deficiency. Usually asymptomatic but may require monitoring.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
